From 195c2e3c59e304e0f321fca640e971f4b8606e51 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 23:11:52 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 223/225] perf: produce the RT low resolution texture copies on
 one worker, patch the records later

Report 06c S3. CheckStoreTextureInPool asked CTexture::GetLowResSystemCopy for up
to six texture roles per material at 256x256 - a synchronous DDS read plus a BC
decompress - from every voxelization job, inside the soup read lock. Stock asked
for one role at 32x32 from one thread. A 300 material level is ~900 cold file
loads spread over every core, which saturates the disk queue and leaves nothing
for anything else on the machine.

- one dedicated worker (SvoRTTexPrefetch) now owns every cache miss. A producer
  asks; if the copy is not there it gets "not ready", the request is queued once,
  and the material is emitted with no texture for that slot.
- because a material's RECORD COUNT does not depend on its texture copies - only
  the slot ids and the atlas UV scale do - the record set can be rebuilt and
  written back over exactly the same records once the copies land. BuildStaticBVH
  registers a patch per deferred material; CSvoEnv::RTApplyMatPatches rebuilds up
  to four per frame on the main thread, writes them in place and marks their
  slices dirty. Until then the cell traces the material's tint, which is what it
  did for an unavailable texture before.
- a patch is kept after it has been applied because it owns the atlas references
  its records point at; both go when the chunk is reclaimed.
- lock order: the patch list lock is always taken INSIDE the atlas lock, so the
  apply pass detaches its work first and never holds both the other way round.
- the normals slot waits for its smoothness donor too: that donor is baked into
  the slice, and a slice written with a flat 255 could not be told apart later.
- e_svoTI_RT_TexPrefetch 0 restores the old synchronous behaviour.

